  POSITION: Hegel's universalism was produced by and against colonial
  history it could not acknowledge---an internal contradiction requiring
  critical reconstruction.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel-Haiti, master-slave-dialectic, recognition, High},
  abstract = {Buck-Morss draws new connections between history, inequality, social conflict, and human emancipation through a fundamental reinterpretation of Hegel's master-slave dialectic. Historicizing the thought of Hegel and the actions taken in the Haitian Revolution, Buck-Morss examines the startling connections between the two and challenges us to widen the boundaries of our historical imagination},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{ng2024fanon,
  author = {Ng, Karen},
  title = {Fanon and {H}egel on the Recognition of Humanity},
  journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {45},
  pages = {571--597},
  doi = {10.1017/hgl.2024.25},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ng argues that Fanon develops a theory of recognition that
  appropriates central Hegelian ideas while transforming them: where Hegel's
  recognition dialectic assumes a formal symmetry between self-consciousnesses,
  Fanon's colonial situation forecloses this symmetry. Ng reconstructs three
  senses of "universal humanity" in Fanon's work---false, abstract, and
  concrete universal---arguing that Fanon's concrete universal provides a
  normative orientation for recognition that avoids essentialism while
  demanding the creation of a "world of reciprocal recognitions."

  RELEVANCE: This recent article provides the most philosophically careful
  analysis of exactly how and where Fanon modifies Hegel's recognition
  theory. It is directly relevant to the question of whether Hegelian
  recognition can survive the colonial critique or requires transformation,
  and what that transformation looks like philosophically.

  POSITION: Fanon transforms rather than simply rejects Hegel's recognition
  theory, developing a concretely universal account that avoids both
  Hegel's abstraction and colonial essentialism.
  },
  keywords = {Fanon-Hegel, recognition, colonialism, High},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
 This paper defends an interpretation of Fanon's theory of recognition as revolving around his claim that we have a basic right to demand human behaviour from the other. Developing key Hegelian ideas in a novel direction, I argue that Fanon's theory of recognition employs a concretely universal concept of humanity as a normative orientation for establishing what he calls a ‘world of reciprocal recognitions’, which he equates with the creation of a ‘human reality’. In the first section, I take up the three passages from Hegel's Phenomenology of Spirit cited by Fanon in Black Skin, White Masks to outline three key features of Fanon's theory of recognition. In section two, I argue that there are three senses of ‘universal humanity’ operative in Fanon's work: a false universal, an abstract universal, and a concrete universal. Whereas the first two are critical, pejorative uses, the third provides the normative orientation for his account of recognition and social struggle. In the third section, I show how Fanon combines features of Hegel's concrete universal with features of Sartre's existential humanism in order to avoid an essentialist or ahistorical approach to human nature. Specifically, I argue that the ideas of self-transcendence and a universal human condition shed light on what Fanon refers to as the right of reciprocal recognition to demand human behaviour from the other, and our one human duty to not renounce our freedom.},
}

@article{faustino2021fanon,
  author = {Faustino, Deivison},
  title = {Frantz {F}anon and the Creolization of {H}egel: Colonialism, the Interdiction of Dialectics and Emancipation},
  journal = {The CLR James Journal},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {27},
  pages = {189--212},
  doi = {10.5840/clrjames2021121489},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Faustino argues that Fanon's engagement with Hegel constitutes
  a "creolization" of the dialectic: Fanon takes the Hegelian framework and
  transforms it from the standpoint of the colonized, showing that colonialism
  "interdicts" the normal operation of the dialectic---the colonized cannot
  achieve recognition through Hegel's route because the colonial situation
  forecloses mutual recognition as a lived possibility. Emancipation requires
  not just political liberation but the creation of genuinely new dialectical
  possibilities.

  RELEVANCE: This article provides a Brazilian Fanonist reading that situates
  Fanon's engagement with Hegel within the broader politics of anticolonial
  struggle, connecting CLR James's tradition to Fanon's transformation of
  Hegel. It contributes to the debate about whether Hegelian dialectics are
  redeemable or constitutively colonial for the enslaved/colonized.

  POSITION: Fanon's "creolization" of Hegel transforms rather than merely
  applies the dialectic, showing that colonial experience requires new
  theoretical forms.
  },
  keywords = {Fanon-Hegel, dialectics, creolization, Medium},
  abstract = {<jats:p />},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{birdpollan2012fanon,
  author = {Bird-Pollan, Stefan},
  title = {Fanon: Colonialism and the Critical Ideals of {G}erman {I}dealism},
  journal = {Critical Horizons},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {13},
  pages = {377--399},
  doi = {10.1558/crit.v13i3.377},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Bird-Pollan argues that Fanon can be productively situated
  within the tradition of German Idealism: Fanon inherits from Kant and
  especially Hegel the conception of agency as something to be achieved
  through struggle rather than given as a natural endowment. This makes
  Fanon's project not a repudiation of German Idealism but its radicalization
  from the position of the colonized, revealing what idealist agency looks
  like when universal claims are taken seriously in colonial conditions.

  RELEVANCE: This article represents the "immanent critique" approach to
  Fanon and German Idealism, arguing that Fanon uses idealist resources
  against idealism's failure to include the colonized. It directly addresses
  the question of whether German Idealism provides resources for anti-colonial
  thought or whether it must be rejected wholesale---the central methodological
  question of Domain 6.

  POSITION: Fanon radicalizes German Idealism rather than rejecting it,
  showing what Kantian-Hegelian agency requires when extended to colonized
  subjects.
  },
  keywords = {Fanon, German-idealism, Hegel-Kant, Medium},
  abstract = {Abstract I argue that Franz Fanon can usefully be situated in the tradition of German Idealism in the sense that he takes from Kant and especially Hegel the conception of agency as something to be achieved through struggle for the ideal of humanity as self-determining. Fanon sees the suffering cased by colonial rule in Africa and elsewhere as deriving from the systematic deprivation of agency by the colonial power. Using the work of Hegel, Fanon seeks to reconstruct the emancipatory project of the black man in close analogy to Hegel’s master-slave dialectic. The violence which Fanon sees as unavoidable in such a struggle is not violence for the sake of violence but, following Hegel, the violence that constitutes the subject in the first place.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{allais2016kants,
  author = {Allais, Lucy},
  title = {Kant's Racism},
  journal = {Philosophical Papers},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {45},
  number = {1--2},
  pages = {1--36},
  doi = {10.1080/05568641.2016.1199170},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Allais argues that Kant's racism is genuine, substantial,
  and widespread across his writings, and cannot be dismissed as mere social
  prejudice of the time. However, she argues against Mills's stronger claim
  that Kant's racism is integral to his moral philosophy: the racism operates
  primarily in Kant's empirical anthropology and physical geography, not in
  the core transcendental and moral philosophy. This permits a distinction
  between the empirical Kant (deeply racist) and the critical Kant (whose
  universalism remains intact), while acknowledging the empirical-critical
  relation is not clean.

  RELEVANCE: Allais occupies a middle position in the debate between full
  integration (Eze, Mills) and full separation (Wood) of Kant's racism from
  his philosophy, making her argument essential for understanding the
  structure of the Kant-race debate. Her account of what "racism" means
  in Kant's context is philosophically careful and widely cited.

  POSITION: Kant's racism is genuine but primarily located in his empirical
  anthropology rather than his critical philosophy, permitting partial
  rehabilitation of the latter.
  },
  keywords = {Kant-race, racism, anthropology, High},
  abstract = {After a long period of comparative neglect, in the last few decades growing numbers of philosophers have been paying attention to the startling contrast presented between Kant’s universal moral theory, with its inspiring enlightenment ideas of human autonomy, equality and dignity and Kant’s racism. Against Charles Mills, who argues that the way to make Kant consistent is by attributing to him a threshold notion of moral personhood, according to which some races do not qualify for consideration under the categorical imperative, I argue that Kant cannot be made consistent on race, and that rather than trying to make him so, we should use the example of Kant’s racism to tell us something about the nature of racism. I argue that Kant’s own moral philosophy and moral psychology in fact give some materials for thinking about his racism, and about racism.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{allen2016end,
  author = {Allen, Amy},
  title = {The End of Progress: Decolonizing the Normative Foundations of Critical Theory},
  year = {2016},
  publisher = {Columbia University Press},
  doi = {10.7312/alle17324},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Allen argues that Frankfurt School critical theory is
  committed to a philosophy of historical progress---derived from the Kantian-
  Hegelian tradition---that is entangled with coloniality and Eurocentrism.
  Against this, she proposes a "metanormative contextualism" that retains
  critical theory's emancipatory aims while abandoning its progressivist
  historiography. The book engages Quijano, Mignolo, and postcolonial theory
  to articulate what a decolonized critical theory would look like.

  RELEVANCE: Allen's book is the most sustained philosophical attempt to
  think through the implications of postcolonial critique for the Kantian-
  Hegelian tradition within critical theory. It directly addresses the
  question of whether the normative foundations of Enlightenment philosophy
  can survive decolonization, and her "metanormative contextualism" answer
  is a major position in current debates.

  POSITION: Critical theory's progressivist foundations must be abandoned
  while its emancipatory aims can be preserved through metanormative
  contextualism.
  },
  keywords = {critical-theory, progress, decolonial, Enlightenment, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{bhambra2014postcolonial,
